>
  <p><strong>No.</strong> A Traverse capability does not get an ambient filesystem. It cannot assume a current directory, a path it may <code>open</code>, or a disk that happens to look the same on every machine.</p>

  <h2>What it gets instead</h2>
  <p>Today’s execution I/O is <strong>stdin/stdout JSON through the host</strong>. The host stages a payload the contract accepts, the capability runs, and the host reads a JSON result or a structured failure back. That channel is described in <a href="/questions/what-io-boundary-do-capabilities-use-today.html">what I/O boundary capabilities use today</a>. Mesh bytes, tables, and other payloads ride inside that JSON shape. They do not arrive as files the guest opened itself.</p>

  <h2>Who owns the disk</h2>
  <p>Hosts are thin clients. They do UI, and they do the I/O — including WASI/WIT when that host implements it. The capability holds the non-UI business rule: the price, the eligibility check, the codec, the policy gate. <a href="/questions/where-does-business-logic-live-in-traverse.html">Where business logic lives</a> is the split. <a href="/blog/where-business-logic-lives.html">The narrative</a> walks the same line. A capability that reaches out for files, environment variables, or the network on its own has left that split.</p>

  <p>A contract may name a specific host call. The host implements that call. The capability invokes the name. That is still host-owned I/O, the same idea as <a href="/questions/can-traverse-capabilities-call-native-os-apis.html">native OS APIs</a>. It is not permission for the guest to wander the filesystem.</p>

  <h2>Check the host before you generalize</h2>
  <p>Discover → execute → trace stays the loop on every host that has shipped. Which hosts have shipped, and in what shape, is <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a>. Do not read this page as "every language embedder exposes the same filesystem API." Read it as: the capability does not have one.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
